{"type":"message-part","timestamp":"2026-07-02T12:05:38.942Z","messageId":"2ff4c325-476d-4d68-9d5e-bf05026ce872","role":"assistant","index":13,"part":{"type":"reasoning","text":"**Developing Upsert Logic**\n\nI'm currently focused on building the `upsert.js` script. My immediate goal is to integrate `PrismaClient` via CommonJS and configure the `upsert` operation, specifically targeting the `email` field for the `'upsert@example.com'` record.\n\n**Refining Upsert Functionality**\n\nI'm solidifying the `upsert` operation. My immediate focus is on executing the `upsert` function sequentially, leveraging the `email` as the unique identifier. The first call should create the record, and the subsequent call, with the identical email but distinct name, will verify the update.\n\n**Implementing Upsert Test**\n\nI'm now writing the `upsert.js` script. It will execute `prisma.user.upsert` twice sequentially. The first call creates the user with email 'upsert@example.com' and name 'First Run'. The second call updates the name to 'Second Run' to verify the upsert logic.\n\n**Verifying Upsert Cleanliness**\n\nI'm considering if a pre-run deletion of the target user is necessary for a truly clean demonstration. The goal is to confirm the update on the second run, and I'm questioning if the current setup guarantees that the user doesn't exist prior to the first upsert operation.\n\n","state":"done"}}
